.TH ATLAS-OPENSSL 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-openssl \- A crypto toolbox: hashes, encoding, symmetric encryption, certificates and keys, from one command.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
openssl  dgst  \-sha256  file
   |       |      |        '\-\-\- input (stdin if omitted)
   |       |      '\-\-\- algorithm option
   |       '\-\-\- sub\-command (dgst, enc, x509, req, rsa, base64, rand ...)
   '\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Hash a string or file; compare with a checksum.
.IP \(bu 2
Encode/decode base64; try AES with a known key.
.IP \(bu 2
Read a certificate or CSR (picoCTF 'ReadMyCert').
.IP \(bu 2
Check RSA keys in a challenge.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fBdgst \-sha256 / \-md5\fR
Hash input.
.br
Use it when: Checksums, flag verification.
.TP
\fBbase64 [\-d]\fR
Base64 encode / decode.
.br
Use it when: Quick encoding.
.TP
\fBenc \-aes\-256\-cbc \-pbkdf2 \-k PASS\fR
Encrypt with a password (adds a random salt).
.br
Use it when: Lab encryption.
.TP
\fBenc \-aes\-128\-ecb \-K HEX \-nosalt\fR
Raw AES with a hex key and no salt.
.br
Use it when: Study modes such as ECB.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR
Decrypt (with \fBenc\fR).
.br
Use it when: Reverse the above.
.TP
\fBx509 \-in cert \-noout \-subject \-dates\fR
Show certificate fields.
.br
Use it when: Read a certificate.
.TP
\fBreq \-x509 \-newkey rsa:2048 \-nodes\fR
Make a self\-signed certificate.
.br
Use it when: Lab TLS.
.TP
\fBrsa \-in key \-text \-noout\fR
Show RSA key parameters.
.br
Use it when: RSA challenges (n, e).
.TP
\fBrand \-hex N\fR
N random bytes as hex.
.br
Use it when: Keys and test data.
.TP
\fBs_client \-connect host:443\fR
Talk TLS to a server.
.br
Use it when: Check a certificate chain.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Hash a string (and avoid the newline trap)"
\fBecho\fR adds a newline unless you use \fB\-n\fR. A different input gives a completely different hash.
.PP
.nf
$ echo \-n hello | openssl dgst \-sha256; echo hello | openssl dgst \-sha256
.fi
.PP
.nf
SHA2\-256(stdin)= 2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824
SHA2\-256(stdin)= 5891b5b522d5df086d0ff0b110fbd9d21bb4fc7163af34d08286a2e846f6be03
.fi
.PP
Two different hashes for what looks like the same word. This is a common source of 'wrong hash' errors in CTFs.
.SS "2. Base64 both ways"
Base64 is an encoding (anyone can reverse it), not encryption.
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'hello world' | openssl base64; echo \-\-\-; cat data.b64 | openssl base64 \-d; echo
.fi
.PP
.nf
aGVsbG8gd29ybGQ=
\-\-\-
hello world
.fi
.PP
Encoding the text gives a string ending in \fB=\fR; decoding \fBdata.b64\fR returns \fBhello world\fR.
.SS "3. Encrypt with a password and look at the file"
With a password, OpenSSL writes the header \fBSalted__\fR and 8 salt bytes, then the ciphertext.
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'secret message' | openssl enc \-aes\-256\-cbc \-pbkdf2 \-k letmein \-out enc.bin; head \-c 16 enc.bin | xxd; openssl enc \-d \-aes\-256\-cbc \-pbkdf2 \-k letmein \-in enc.bin; echo
.fi
.PP
.nf
00000000: 5361 6c74 6564 5f5f 0a99 1a9c 4af5 86f1  Salted__....J...
secret message
.fi
.PP
The first bytes spell \fBSalted__\fR; decryption with the same password returns the message. A random salt means two runs give different files.
.SS "4. ECB mode leaks patterns"
ECB encrypts each 16\-byte block independently, so equal plaintext blocks give equal ciphertext blocks.
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA' | openssl enc \-aes\-128\-ecb \-K 000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f \-nosalt | xxd \-c 16
.fi
.PP
.nf
00000000: dd4b 1a0b 47da a706 7d0b 59d9 5d58 a6ae  .K..G...}.Y.]X..
00000010: dd4b 1a0b 47da a706 7d0b 59d9 5d58 a6ae  .K..G...}.Y.]X..
00000020: 954f 64f2 e4e8 6e9e ee82 d202 1668 4899  .Od...n......hH.
.fi
.PP
The first two ciphertext lines are identical because the plaintext had two identical blocks (the third line is padding). This is exactly the weakness behind Natas 28 and the CyberSci block\-swapping challenge.
.SS "5. Read a certificate"
First we create a throw\-away self\-signed certificate, then read its fields with \fBx509 \-noout\fR.
.PP
.nf
$ openssl req \-x509 \-newkey rsa:2048 \-nodes \-keyout k.pem \-out c.pem \-subj '/CN=demo.local/O=Lab' \-days 1 2>/dev/null; openssl x509 \-in c.pem \-noout \-subject \-issuer
.fi
.PP
.nf
subject=CN = demo.local, O = Lab
issuer=CN = demo.local, O = Lab
.fi
.PP
Subject and issuer are the same because it is self\-signed. Add \fB\-dates\fR or \fB\-text\fR to see validity and the key.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
\fBecho\fR without \fB\-n\fR hashes a trailing newline.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-pbkdf2\fR and \fB\-md sha256\fR differ across OpenSSL versions: decrypting old files may need the original options.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBopenssl enc\fR is for study: never design real encryption with raw \fBenc\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
CSR vs certificate vs key are different PEM types: check the header line (\fB\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN ...\fR).
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Encrypt a 48\-byte message of repeating blocks with ECB and with CBC and compare the dumps.
.IP 2. 3
Hash the contents of \fBhello.c\fR and verify with \fBsha256sum\fR.
.IP 3. 3
Create a certificate with a different CN and read it back.
.SH SEE ALSO
sha256sum / md5sum, base64, gpg, hashcat, RsaCtfTool, python pycryptodome.
